rsz: recompute design area for a progress row only after an accepted move

Candidate fix from the repair_timing runtime study, carried here while
it is proposed upstream; retires at the bump onto an OpenROAD that
carries it. Identical to the second commit of the upstream pull request.

Every -verbose progress row called computeDesignArea(), a walk over
every instance, and most rows follow passes that accepted no move (on
riscv32i cts 217 of 1181 passes did). The area is recomputed only when
the committer's accepted-move count has changed since the row that
last computed it. The printed value is the same.

Chesterton's Fence: Resizer::design_area_ is meant to be incremental,
but buffer insertion does not go through designAreaIncr(), so
designArea() re-derives it on every call and cannot be trusted here.
Making it trustworthy is a larger change across every instance-creating
path; caching by move count is the local step.

